course: history
lesson: 84
title: Gaols, magistrates and old punishments: from the stocks to the Children Act of 1908
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: In «The Wind in the Willows» Toad is tried by magistrates, given twenty years and dragged to the deepest dungeon of an old castle, while the crowd pelts him with carrots. It is a funny, made-up trial, but every piece of it comes from real history.
explain: Long ago people really were locked in the stocks so neighbours could jeer and throw rotten food at them, and some castles really were used as gaols. But by 1908 most prisoners were kept in plain new prisons with rows of cells, and magistrates could never give anyone twenty years.
explain: Over two hundred years, punishments in Britain slowly became less cruel. Reformers like Elizabeth Fry visited prisoners and showed that people could be helped to change.
explain: In the very year the book came out, 1908, a new law stopped children under 14 being sent to prison and set up special courts for young people.
explain: So when Toad's trial makes you laugh, ask: what would a real court have done, then and now?
fact: «Gaol» is an old spelling of «jail» and is said exactly the same way; a gaoler is a jailer, the person who keeps the prisoners, and old gaolers often lived in the gaol with their families.
fact: Magistrates are ordinary local people, not trained lawyers, who judge smaller cases in England and Wales; they still do this today, and they can only give short prison sentences of months, not years.
fact: Serious cases go to a higher court with a judge and a jury; so twenty years from a bench of magistrates, as Toad gets, could not really happen.
fact: Long ago a common punishment was the stocks, a wooden frame that locked a person's feet, so that people could laugh at them and throw rotten food; the stocks were still used now and then until the 1870s.
fact: From 1788 to 1868 Britain sent about 160,000 convicts on ships to Australia, a punishment called transportation, sometimes for quite small thefts.
fact: In many Victorian prisons prisoners did hard labour, such as walking for hours on a treadwheel, a huge turning wheel of steps, which is where the word «treadmill» comes from.
fact: Some castles really were prisons: the Tower of London held famous prisoners for hundreds of years, and Lancaster Castle was still used as a prison until 2011.
fact: By 1908 most prisoners were held in purpose-built prisons with long rows of small cells, not in castle dungeons; prison food was plain, like bread, porridge and potatoes.
fact: Elizabeth Fry began visiting women prisoners in Newgate prison in London in 1813, taught them to sew and read, started a school for their children, and was later shown on the five-pound note.
fact: The Children Act of 1908, the year the book came out, stopped children under 14 being sent to prison and set up special courts for young people.
fact: Paying a guard or helper to let a prisoner escape is bribery and is a crime; in the book the washerwoman takes gold coins and asks to be tied up so it looks as if she was attacked.
fact: Today the law says a punishment should fit the crime; being rude to a police officer is wrong, but in the book it gets the longest sentence of all, fifteen years, which is the joke.
exercise: How do you say the word «gaol»? | The same as «jail» | Like «goal» in football | Like «gale», a strong wind | Like «gall», with a short sound
exercise: Who are magistrates? | Ordinary local people who judge smaller cases | Trained lawyers who judge only the worst murders | Police officers who decide all the punishments | The King's officers who collect the taxes
exercise: What sentences can magistrates give? | Only short ones, months not years | Any sentence, even twenty years or more | Only fines, never any prison at all | Only the death sentence
exercise: Where do serious cases go? | A higher court with a judge and a jury | Back to the police station for a vote | To the town's mayor, who decides alone | To the prisoner's own family to judge
exercise: What were the stocks? | A wooden frame that locked a person's feet | A cupboard where the gaoler kept his money | A row of cells in a prison | A kind of soup for prisoners
exercise: Why were people put in the stocks? | So that others could jeer at them | To keep their feet warm in winter | So they could rest after a long walk | To measure how tall they were
exercise: What was transportation as a punishment? | Being sent by ship to Australia | Being made to drive a cart for the king | Being moved from one cell to another every day | Being carried around town on a chair
exercise: About how many convicts were sent to Australia? | About 160,000 | About 1,600 | About 16 | About 16 million
exercise: What was a treadwheel? | A turning wheel of steps walked on as hard labour | A big wheel on the cart that carried prisoners | A spinning wheel for making cloth in the cells | A game with a hoop played in the prison yard
exercise: Which castle was still used as a prison until 2011? | Lancaster Castle | Windsor Castle | Edinburgh Castle | Toad Hall
exercise: Where were most prisoners kept by 1908? | In purpose-built prisons with rows of cells | In the deepest dungeons of old stone castles | In the stocks in the square of every town | On old prison ships out at sea, far from land
exercise: What did Elizabeth Fry do? | Helped women prisoners and their children in Newgate | Built the first and deepest castle dungeon in London | Invented the treadwheel for Victorian prisons | Wrote «The Wind in the Willows» in prison
exercise: What did the Children Act of 1908 do? | Stopped children under 14 being sent to prison | Made all children go to work in factories | Sent children to Australia | Banned children from reading books about toads
exercise: What is bribery? | Paying someone to break the rules for you | Asking a guard very politely to let you out | Dressing up as someone else to escape | Telling a funny story to make a judge laugh
exercise: What does it mean that a punishment should fit the crime? | Small wrongs get small punishments, big wrongs bigger ones | Every crime, big or small, gets exactly the same punishment | The punishment depends mostly on how rich or poor you are | The judge picks a punishment out of a hat
test: «toad got 20 years from the magistrates, that's legit how it worked back then right» What is true? | No, magistrates could only give short sentences of months | Yes, magistrates gave twenty years for almost any crime at all | Yes, but only to toads and other animals | No, magistrates could only give the death sentence
test: The crowd pelts Toad «with jeers, carrots, and popular catch-words». Which old punishment is that like? | The stocks, where people jeered and threw rotten food | Transportation, where prisoners were sent to Australia on ships | The treadwheel | A fine
test: «toad's in a castle dungeon, so prisons in 1908 were castles» What is the best answer? | Most were new prisons with rows of cells, though a few castles were still used | Yes, every prison in 1908 was a castle with a drawbridge and knights | No, prisons in 1908 were all built deep underground | Prisons in 1908 were just tents in fields
test: Toad's sentence: one year for stealing the car, three for driving dangerously, fifteen for cheeking the police. What is wrong with that? | The smallest wrong, rudeness, gets by far the biggest punishment | The theft of the car should have got nothing at all | Driving fast was not a crime at all in those days | Nothing, that is exactly how it should be done
test: A girl of 12 in 1850 steals a loaf. What might have happened to her? | She could have been sent to prison, or even transported | She would have been sent to a special youth court, as today | She would have been given a medal for being hungry | She would have been made a magistrate
test: The same girl steals a loaf in 1910. What is different? | The Children Act of 1908 meant she could not be sent to prison | She would now be transported to Australia on a convict ship | She would now be locked in the stocks for a whole week | Nothing at all had changed for children since 1850
test: «treadmill at the gym, that's from prison?? no way» Is it true? | Yes, Victorian prisoners walked on treadwheels as hard labour | No, the treadmill was invented for gyms in the 1990s | No, a treadmill is a mill that grinds the treads for tyres | Yes, but only in Australia
test: The washerwoman asks to be «gagged and bound» so it looks as if Toad attacked her. Why? | She took his gold coins, which was bribery, and wanted to hide it | She liked being tied up in a corner of the cell | It was the law that every washerwoman be tied up on Fridays | She wanted to have a long rest from washing
test: «my mate says the gaoler's daughter is made up, gaolers didnt have families» What is right? | Old gaolers often lived in the gaol with their families | Gaolers were never allowed to marry or have children | Gaolers were always soldiers who lived in barracks | Only castles had gaolers, and they lived in towers
test: Why might Elizabeth Fry have taught prisoners to sew and read? | So they could earn an honest living and change their lives | So they could escape by sewing rope ladders from sheets | So they could write letters to the King asking to be freed | So they could make all the prison uniforms for free
test: ★★★ Toad gets 1 year, then 3 years, then 15 years, and the Chairman rounds the total up to twenty. If Toad had already served 9 months when he escaped, how many months of the twenty years were left? | 231 | 240 | 219 | 211
exam: Identify the persons who, in England and Wales, adjudicate upon the lesser criminal cases. | Magistrates, being ordinary local persons rather than trained lawyers | Judges of the highest court, sitting together with a jury of twelve citizens | Officers of the police, acting upon their own authority | The clergy of the parish church
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the punishment of transportation. | Some 160,000 convicts were conveyed by ship to Australia between 1788 and 1868 | Prisoners were conveyed by railway to the far north of Scotland and set to work in the mines there | Prisoners were transported to the Moon | Convicts were carried about the town in a cart
exam: Ascertain the principal provision of the Children Act of 1908 concerning imprisonment. | Children under fourteen were no longer to be sent to prison | Children were henceforth to be imprisoned alongside adults in every gaol of the kingdom | All children were to be transported abroad | Children were to serve as gaolers
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the principle that a punishment should fit the crime? | Lesser offences ought to receive lesser punishments and graver offences graver ones | All offences, however slight or however grave, ought to receive identical punishments | The punishment ought to depend upon the wealth of the offender | The punishment ought to be chosen at random by the court
